= Topological vector space
{wiki}

A topological vector space is a <vector space> $E$ over $\mathbb K=\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, equipped with a <topology> such that <vector addition> and <scalar multiplication> are jointly <continuous>:
$$
E\times E\longrightarrow E,\quad (x,y)\longmapsto x+y,
\qquad
\mathbb K\times E\longrightarrow E,\quad (\lambda,x)\longmapsto\lambda x.
$$
The domains carry the <product topology>, and the <scalar> field carries its usual <topology>. Joint <continuity> permits both inputs to vary at once. Here the definition does not impose the <Hausdorff> condition; we state it separately when needed. https://www.math.lmu.de/~philip/publications/lectureNotes/philipPeter_FunctionalAnalysis.pdf[Peter Philip, Functional Analysis, Definition 1.1 and Example 1.2]{ref}.

Every <normed vector space> is a <topological vector space>: the <triangle inequality> gives $\|x+y\|\leq\|x\|+\|y\|$, while
$$
\|\lambda x-\lambda_0x_0\|\leq |\lambda|\,\|x-x_0\|+|\lambda-\lambda_0|\,\|x_0\|
$$
controls <scalar multiplication> near any $(\lambda_0,x_0)$. More generally, a <seminorm topology> supplies many measurements of smallness, rather than one <norm>. Translation by any <vector> is a <homeomorphism>, so a <neighbourhood basis> at zero determines all <neighbourhoods> by translation.

The compatibility matters: on a nonzero real <vector space>, the <discrete topology> makes <vector addition> <continuous>, but not <scalar multiplication>. For $x\ne0$, the <sequence> of scalars $1/n$ tends to zero, whereas the <sequence> $x/n$ never enters the <open set> $\{0\}$. Thus choosing a <topology> arbitrarily need not respect the <linear algebra>.

This framework lets <functional analysis> choose the appropriate notion of <limit of a sequence>. <Norm topology> measures one global error, <weak topology> tests <linear functionals>, and the <Fréchet space> of <smooth functions> controls every order of <derivative>. In each case the operations forming <linear combinations> are <continuous>, making approximation compatible with the underlying equations.

= Dense subspace
{parent=Topological vector space}
{title2=$\overline D=X$}

A dense subspace of a <topological vector space> $X$ is a <linear subspace> $D$ whose closure is all of $X$. It permits continuous operators to be determined or extended from values on $D$. For a normed space, every vector in $X$ is a limit of vectors from $D$.

= Sequential continuity criterion in a metrizable vector space
{parent=Topological vector space}

A linear map from a <metrizable space> carrying a vector-space topology is continuous if it takes every null sequence to a null sequence. If continuity fails, choose a point in each shrinking basic neighborhood whose image stays outside a fixed neighborhood of zero. These points converge to zero but their images do not. For a <Fréchet space> described by increasing <seminorms> $p_j$, a discontinuous functional admits $p_j(f_j)\leq1/j$ and $|u(f_j)|\geq1$.

= Bounded set in a topological vector space
{parent=Topological vector space}

= Bounded sets in a topological vector space
{synonym}

A subset $B$ of a <topological vector space> is bounded when every neighborhood $U$ of zero absorbs it: $B\subseteq tU$ for all sufficiently large positive $t$. In a <locally convex space> defined by <seminorms> $p_\lambda$, this is equivalent to $\sup_{x\in B}p_\lambda(x)<\infty$ for every $\lambda$. In particular, a bounded subset of the <Schwartz space> has a uniform bound for every Schwartz seminorm; it need not have uniformly bounded supports.

= Strict inductive limit topology
{parent=Topological vector space}

A strict inductive limit topology on an increasing union $E=\bigcup_nE_n$ is the finest locally convex topology compatible with the inclusions of the stages $E_n$. A linear map from $E$ is continuous exactly when each restriction to $E_n$ is continuous.

= Metrizable topological vector space
{parent=Topological vector space}

A topological vector space is metrizable when its topology is induced by a <metric>. At the origin it then has a countable neighborhood basis, so continuity of a <linear map> can be tested on sequences.

= Finite-dimensional vector-space topology
{parent=Topological vector space}

Every finite-dimensional real vector space has a unique Hausdorff topology making it a topological vector space. Choosing a basis identifies it with ordinary Euclidean space; every linear map between such spaces is continuous.

= Equivalence of norms in finite dimensions
{parent=Finite-dimensional vector-space topology}

On a finite-dimensional vector space over a complete valued field, any two norms induce the same topology. Choosing a basis reduces the claim to comparison with the maximum of the absolute values of the coordinates.

= Sharp comparison of l1 and l2 norms
{parent=Equivalence of norms in finite dimensions}
{title2=$\|x\|_2\leq\|x\|_1\leq\sqrt n\|x\|_2$}

For $x\in\mathbb R^n$, <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> applied to $(|x_i|)$ and $(1)$ gives the upper bound. Equal absolute coordinate values attain its factor $\sqrt n$. Expanding $(\sum_i|x_i|)^2$ gives the lower bound, and a vector with only one nonzero coordinate attains its factor one. Thus both constants are optimal. These <Lp norms> define the same finite-dimensional topology despite having different unit balls.

= Locally convex space
{parent=Topological vector space}
{wiki}

A locally convex space is a <topological vector space> with a <neighbourhood basis> at zero consisting of <convex sets>. Equivalently, its <topology> is a <seminorm topology> generated by some family $\mathcal P$ of <seminorms>. We allow non-<Hausdorff> spaces here; a <separating family of seminorms> gives precisely the <Hausdorff> case. Every <normed vector space> is locally convex, but <smooth convergence> generally requires a whole family of measurements.

The geometric and measurement descriptions fit together: <seminorm> sublevel sets are <convex>, while the <Minkowski functional> of an open, convex zero <neighbourhood> invariant under all <scalar multiplications> with $|\lambda|\leq1$ is a <continuous> <seminorm>. Such <neighbourhoods> can be chosen as a <neighbourhood basis>. https://www.math.lmu.de/~philip/publications/lectureNotes/philipPeter_FunctionalAnalysis.pdf[Peter Philip, Functional Analysis, Theorem 1.40, equivalence of local convexity and seminorm topologies]{ref}.

Local convexity makes <Hahn-Banach theorem> and <separation of a point and an open convex set> available. It turns geometric distinctions into <continuous linear functionals>, connecting <convex sets> to the <continuous dual space>. For a <seminorm topology> generated by $\mathcal P$, a <linear functional> $\ell$ is <continuous> exactly when there are $p_1,\ldots,p_m\in\mathcal P$ and $C>0$ with
$$
|\ell(x)|\leq C\max_{1\leq i\leq m}p_i(x)\qquad(x\in E).
$$
Thus one <continuous linear functional> needs only finitely many of the available measurements. For example, <point evaluation> of a <derivative> on the <space of smooth functions on a compact interval> is controlled by a suitable <derivative seminorm>.

= Seminorm topology
{parent=Locally convex space}
{title2=$\tau_{\mathcal P}$}

Given a nonempty family $\mathcal P$ of finite-valued <seminorms> on a real or complex <vector space> $E$, its seminorm topology has basic open zero <neighbourhoods>
$$
U(p_1,\ldots,p_m;\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_m)
=\bigcap_{i=1}^m\{x\in E:p_i(x)<\varepsilon_i\},
\qquad p_i\in\mathcal P,\quad\varepsilon_i>0.
$$
Their translates form a <basis of a topology>. Only finite <intersections> are required: an infinite <intersection> might impose a stronger <topology>. Equivalently, put $q(x)=\max_i p_i(x)/\varepsilon_i$; then $q$ is a <seminorm> and $U=\{q<1\}$. See https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gue/lehre/25AFA/AFA.pdf[Günther Hörmann, Advanced Functional Analysis, section 4, neighbourhood systems from seminorms] for the general construction.

Each defining <seminorm> is <continuous>, since $|p(x)-p(y)|\leq p(x-y)$. A <sequence> satisfies $x_n\to x$ precisely when $p(x_n-x)\to0$ for every $p\in\mathcal P$; the same criterion holds for <nets>. Finitely many measurements can each meet a prescribed tolerance after a common index.

The <seminorm> axioms also establish the required joint <continuity> of <vector addition> and <scalar multiplication>:
$$
p\bigl((x+y)-(x_0+y_0)\bigr)\leq p(x-x_0)+p(y-y_0),
\qquad
p(\lambda x-\lambda_0x_0)\leq |\lambda|p(x-x_0)+|\lambda-\lambda_0|p(x_0).
$$
For a basic <neighbourhood>, apply these estimates to its finitely many <seminorms>; near $\lambda_0$ the <absolute value> $|\lambda|$ is bounded. This gives a <locally convex space> even when some directions are invisible to every measurement.

= Separating family of seminorms
{parent=Seminorm topology}
{title2=$\bigcap_{p\in\mathcal P}\ker p=\{0\}$}

A family of <seminorms> separates points when every nonzero <vector> $z$ has $p(z)>0$ for some member $p$. Equivalently, its <kernels of seminorms> have intersection $\{0\}$, or any two distinct <vectors> $x,y$ have $p(x-y)>0$ for some $p$. Individual <seminorms> can have large <kernels of seminorms>; the whole family must have no common invisible direction.

The associated <seminorm topology> is <Hausdorff> if and only if the family separates points. Indeed, if $p(x-y)=a>0$, the <neighbourhoods> $\{u:p(u-x)<a/3\}$ and $\{v:p(v-y)<a/3\}$ are disjoint by the <triangle inequality>. Conversely, if every $p$ vanishes on a nonzero $z$, then zero and $z$ belong to exactly the same open <sets> and cannot be separated. This criterion keeps separation distinct from <completeness>: detecting all directions does not guarantee that all <Cauchy sequences> have limits.

= Compact metrizable convex set
{parent=Locally convex space}

Here the set is a nonempty compact metrizable convex subset of a Hausdorff locally convex space. The ambient space can be infinite-dimensional. Continuous affine <functions> separate its points, and weak-star compact dual balls with separable preduals provide examples. This general setting is needed for <Choquet theorem>.

= Choquet theorem
{c}
{parent=Compact metrizable convex set}

= Choquet's theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Every point of a compact metrizable convex subset of a Hausdorff locally convex space is the <barycenter> of a <Borel probability measure> concentrated on its <extreme points>. The theorem is an existence assertion; uniqueness requires additional hypotheses such as a simplex structure.

= Threshold Choquet representation in L-infinity
{parent=Choquet theorem}
{title2=$H_t(s)=2\,1_{\{t\le(1+f(s))/2\}}-1$}

For a real <function> in the $L^\infty$ unit ball, push uniform <measure> on $t\in[0,1]$ forward by the displayed sign-valued <functions>. In the <weak-star topology> this is a Borel probability concentrated on the <extreme points>, and <Fubini's theorem> shows that its <barycenter> is $f$. Weak-star compactness and separability of the predual $L^1$ place the example within <Choquet theorem>.

= Choquet's theorem by strict convexity
{c}
{parent=Choquet theorem}

Maximize the integral of a continuous strictly convex <function> among <probability measures> with a fixed <barycenter>. The <supporting measure lemma for affine upper envelopes> implies that the maximizing <measure> has zero integral of the nonnegative envelope gap. Strict convexity makes this gap positive at every nonextreme point, so the <measure> is concentrated on the extreme boundary. Metrizability makes that boundary Borel and supplies the continuous strictly convex <function>.

= Affine upper envelope
{parent=Compact metrizable convex set}
{title2=$\overline f(x)=\inf\{a(x):a\in A(K),\ a\ge f\}$}

For a bounded real <function> on a compact convex set, this envelope is the infimum of its continuous affine majorants. It is finite, concave and <upper semicontinuous>, and dominates the <function>. For continuous <functions> it describes the maximal integral among <probability measures> with a specified <barycenter>. Its homogeneity, subadditivity and affine-translation identities give the <supporting measure lemma for affine upper envelopes>.

= Supporting measure lemma for affine upper envelopes
{parent=Affine upper envelope}
{title2=$\nu(f)=\mu(\overline f),\quad\nu(g)\le\mu(\overline g)\ \forall g\in C(K)$}

The sublinear functional $p(g)=\mu(\overline g)$ has a linear supporting functional taking the value $p(f)$ at a specified continuous $f$, by the <Hahn-Banach theorem>. It is positive and normalized because $p$ has these values on constants. The <Riesz-Markov-Kakutani representation theorem> gives the probability $\nu$. Testing affine <functions> and their negatives shows that $\mu$ and $\nu$ have the same <barycenter>.

= Barycenter
{parent=Compact metrizable convex set}
{title2=$a(b_\mu)=\int a\,d\mu\quad(a\text{ continuous affine})$}

= Barycentre
{synonym}

The <barycenter> of a <probability measure> on a compact convex set is the point satisfying the displayed affine integral identities. It exists by approximating the <measure> by finitely supported <measures> and using compactness of their finite convex combinations. It is unique because continuous affine <functions> separate points. In a Banach-space setting it agrees with the appropriate vector integral when that integral is defined.

= Seminorm
{title2=$p$}
{parent=Locally convex space}
{wiki}

A seminorm on a real or complex <vector space> $E$ is a finite-valued <function> $p:E\to[0,\infty)$ satisfying absolute homogeneity and the <triangle inequality>:
$$
p(\lambda x)=|\lambda|p(x),\qquad p(x+y)\leq p(x)+p(y)
\qquad(x,y\in E,\ \lambda\in\mathbb K).
$$
In particular $p(0)=0$ and $p(-x)=p(x)$. A <seminorm> is a <norm> exactly when $p(x)=0$ implies $x=0$: this is the extra <definiteness of a norm> axiom. The zero <function> is a valid <seminorm>. The missing definiteness is useful when a measurement deliberately ignores some directions, such as an individual coordinate or the constant term of a <function> whose <derivative> is measured.

For example, on $C^1([0,1])$ the <function> $p(f)=\sup_{t\in[0,1]}|f'(t)|$ is a <seminorm> that vanishes on every constant <function>. Adding the value measurement gives the <norm> $q(f)=|f(0)|+p(f)$: if $q(f)=0$, the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives $f(t)=f(0)+\int_0^t f'(s)\,ds=0$. A family of such partial measurements defines a <seminorm topology>.

= Kernel of a seminorm
{parent=Seminorm}
{title2=$\ker p=\{x:p(x)=0\}$}

= Kernels of seminorms
{synonym}

The kernel of a <seminorm> is its zero set, a <linear subspace> by absolute homogeneity and the <triangle inequality>. The estimate $|p(x)-p(y)|\leq p(x-y)$ shows that adding an element of $\ker p$ does not change $p$. Hence $\|x+\ker p\|=p(x)$ defines a <norm> on the <quotient vector space> $E/\ker p$.

On $E$ itself, $d_p(x,y)=p(x-y)$ is a <pseudometric>; it is a <metric> exactly when $p$ is a <norm>. In particular, zero distance in one <pseudometric> need not mean equality of <vectors>. A <separating family of seminorms> removes the common zero-distance ambiguity.

= Coordinate seminorms
{parent=Seminorm}
{title2=$p_x(x,y)=|x|,\quad p_y(x,y)=|y|$}

On $\mathbb R^2$, the <coordinate seminorms> $p_x$ and $p_y$ measure horizontal and vertical components separately. Their <kernels of seminorms> are the vertical and horizontal axes. Neither is a <norm>, but together they form a <separating family of seminorms>. A basic zero <neighbourhood> is
$$
\{p_x<a\}\cap\{p_y<b\}=(-a,a)\times(-b,b)\qquad(a,b>0).
$$
Each condition alone is an unbounded strip; their intersection is an open rectangle. For $a=b=1$, this is the unit <open ball> of the <maximum norm> $\|(x,y)\|_\infty=\max\{|x|,|y|\}$. For unequal tolerances it is the unit <open ball> of the weighted <norm> $\max\{|x|/a,|y|/b\}$. Thus the two coordinate measurements generate the usual <topology> of the plane.

For a numerical check, take $a=1$, $b=1/2$: $(0.8,0.4)$ passes both tests, $(0.8,0.7)$ passes only the horizontal test, and $(1.2,0.4)$ passes only the vertical test. A direction missed by one <seminorm> is detected by the other.

\Image[seminorm-neighborhoods.png]
{title=Two coordinate seminorm strips intersect in a basic open rectangle}

= Dual seminorm
{title2=$p^*$}
{parent=Seminorm}

Given a bilinear pairing $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ and a <seminorm> $p$, its dual seminorm is
$$
p^*(y)=\sup_{p(x)\leq1}|\langle x,y\rangle|.
$$
It may be infinite when $y$ does not annihilate the kernel of $p$. Whenever it is finite, the defining inequality gives $|\langle x,y\rangle|\leq p(x)p^*(y)$.

= Fréchet space
{c}
{parent=Locally convex space}
{wiki}

A Fréchet space is a <Hausdorff> <locally convex space> whose <topology> is induced by a complete translation-invariant <metric>. Translation invariance means $d(x+z,y+z)=d(x,y)$; <completeness> means every <Cauchy sequence> converges in the space. Use a translation-invariant <metric> for this condition: an arbitrary compatible <metric> can change which <sequences> are <Cauchy sequences>. https://www.math.lmu.de/~philip/publications/lectureNotes/philipPeter_FunctionalAnalysis.pdf[Peter Philip, Functional Analysis, Definition 1.22 and Remark 1.23]{ref}.

Equivalently, the <topology> comes from a countable <separating family of seminorms> $(p_k)_{k\geq0}$ and is complete in their common sense: if $p_k(x_n-x_m)\to0$ as $n,m\to\infty$ for every fixed $k$, then some $x\in E$ satisfies $p_k(x_n-x)\to0$ for every $k$. A useful compatible translation-invariant <metric> is
$$
d(x,y)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}2^{-(k+1)}\min\{1,p_k(x-y)\}.
$$
It is finite because the weights sum to one. The separating condition makes it a <metric>; without that condition the formula gives only a <pseudometric>. Its <convergence in a metric space> and <Cauchy sequence> conditions are exactly the conditions for all the $p_k$ separately: control finitely many terms, then bound the remaining tail by $2^{-(K+1)}$. Replacing $p_k$ by $\max_{0\leq j\leq k}p_j$ gives an increasing family with the same <topology>.

Every <Banach space> is a <Fréchet space>, using its <norm> as the single measurement. The <space of smooth functions on a compact interval> is a <Fréchet space> with <derivative seminorms> but with no single <norm> inducing its smooth <topology>. This is why <Fréchet spaces> are useful in <differential equations>: one can require accuracy in every order of <derivative>, while each particular estimate uses only finitely many orders.

The name honors <Maurice Fréchet>. His doctoral thesis was published as https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF03018603[Sur quelques points du calcul fonctionnel, Rendiconti del Circolo Matematico di Palermo 22, pages 1–72 (1906)]{ref}; an https://zenodo.org/records/1428464[open scan of Fréchet's original paper]{ref} is available. This identifies a historical source without attributing the modern locally convex definition to that paper.

= Derivative seminorms on a compact interval
{parent=Fréchet space}
{title2=$p_k(f)=\max_{0\leq j\leq k}\|f^{(j)}\|_\infty$}

= Derivative seminorm
{synonym}

Here $C^\infty([0,1])$ means the real- or complex-valued <functions> <continuous> on $[0,1]$, <smooth> on $(0,1)$, with every interior <derivative> having a <continuous> extension to the endpoints; endpoint values of <derivatives> are these extensions. On this <space of smooth functions on a compact interval>, define
$$
p_k(f)=\max_{0\leq j\leq k}\sup_{t\in[0,1]}|f^{(j)}(t)|,
\qquad k=0,1,2,\ldots,\qquad f^{(0)}=f.
$$
These finite-valued <seminorms> increase with $k$ and separate points. In fact every $p_k$ is a <norm>, since it includes $p_0(f)=\|f\|_\infty$. The terminology <seminorm> includes <norms>; it does not assert that every measurement has a nontrivial <kernel of a seminorm>. <Smooth convergence> means $p_k(f_n-f)\to0$ for every fixed $k$, equivalently <uniform convergence> of every <derivative> separately. It does not demand one tolerance or one convergence index that works for all orders simultaneously. The maximum convention is equivalent to the <derivative supremum norm> convention using a finite sum, since $p_k(f)\leq\sum_{j=0}^k\|f^{(j)}\|_\infty\leq(k+1)p_k(f)$.

For a worked example, $f(t)=t^3$ gives $p_0(f)=1$, $p_1(f)=3$, $p_2(f)=6$, and $p_k(f)=6$ for $k\geq3$. The <linear functional> $\ell(f)=f''(1/2)$ obeys $|\ell(f)|\leq p_2(f)$ and is <continuous>. Similarly, <differentiation> is a <continuous linear operator> $D:f\mapsto f'$ for <smooth convergence>, because $p_k(Df)\leq p_{k+1}(f)$.

The <space of smooth functions on a compact interval> is complete for this <seminorm topology>. A <Cauchy sequence> has <uniform limits> $g_j$ for its <derivatives> of each order. Passing to the limit in the <fundamental theorem of calculus> identity
$$
f_n^{(j)}(t)-f_n^{(j)}(0)=\int_0^t f_n^{(j+1)}(s)\,ds
$$
gives $g_j(t)-g_j(0)=\int_0^t g_{j+1}(s)\,ds$, so $g_j'=g_{j+1}$ and $g_0$ belongs to the same space. This is the <completeness of the smooth-function metric> argument; the <metric> there and the increasing-$p_k$ <metric> above induce the same smooth <topology> and the same <Cauchy sequences>.

= Smooth convergence on a compact interval
{parent=Derivative seminorms on a compact interval}
{title2=$p_k(f_n-f)\to0\text{ for every }k$}

= Smooth convergence
{synonym}

Smooth convergence on a compact <interval> requires <uniform convergence> of the <functions> and of their <derivatives> of every order separately, as measured by the <derivative seminorms>. The need for several scales is visible in
$$
f_n(t)=\frac{\sin(nt)}n,\qquad p_0(f_n)\leq\frac1n\longrightarrow0,
\qquad f_n'(t)=\cos(nt),\qquad p_1(f_n)=1.
$$
For $n\geq2$, $p_0(f_n)=1/n$ because $t=\pi/(2n)$ lies in $[0,1]$. The <derivative> has <supremum norm> one for every $n$, already at $t=0$. Thus $f_n$ <converges uniformly> to zero, but fails the test for <smooth convergence> to zero. https://www.buttenschoen.ca/MATH725/introduction/ck-spaces/[Andreas Buttenschoen, Applied Functional Analysis, spaces of continuous and differentiable functions] discusses this example and the family of smoothness measurements.

\Image[smooth-function-seminorm-convergence.png]
{title=Shrinking function values coexist with derivative errors of size one}

More generally, for any fixed $K\geq0$, $h_n(t)=\sin(nt)/n^{K+1}$ has $p_K(h_n)\leq1/n$ but $p_{K+1}(h_n)=1$ for $n\geq2$. If a <norm> $N$ induced the smooth <topology>, its <continuity> would give $N(f)\leq C p_K(f)$ for some finite $K$, while <continuity> of $p_{K+1}$ for $N$ would give $p_{K+1}(f)\leq C'N(f)$. Substituting $h_n$ contradicts these two bounds. <Smooth convergence> therefore cannot be reduced to one <norm>. Its beauty is that every finite order can be controlled without fixing a final order in advance.

= Minkowski functional
{c}
{parent=Locally convex space}
{title2=$p_U$}
{wiki}

For an absorbing convex set $U$ containing zero, its Minkowski functional is $p_U(x)=\inf\{t>0:x\in tU\}$. If $U$ is open and convex, then $p_U$ is sublinear and $U=\{x:p_U(x)<1\}$.

= Norm from a bounded symmetric convex neighbourhood
{parent=Minkowski functional}
{title2=$p_U(x)=\inf\{t>0:x\in tU\}$}

For a bounded convex open set $U$ containing zero and invariant under $x\mapsto-x$, its <Minkowski functional> is a <norm>. Inner and outer Euclidean balls give positive finite bounds; symmetry and scaling give absolute homogeneity; convexity gives the <triangle inequality>. Openness makes $U=\{p_U<1\}$, while boundedness prevents nonzero vectors from having zero gauge.

= Separation of a point and an open convex set
{parent=Locally convex space}

If $C$ is a nonempty open convex subset of a real locally convex space and $x_0\notin C$, a continuous linear functional strictly separates them: after choosing a sign, $f(c)<f(x_0)$ for every $c\in C$. Apply the <Hahn-Banach theorem> to the <Minkowski functional> of a translate of $C$.

= Hahn-Banach separation theorem for two convex sets
{c}
{parent=Separation of a point and an open convex set}

If $A$ and $B$ are disjoint nonempty convex subsets of a real locally convex space and $A$ is open, then some continuous linear functional $f$ satisfies
$$
f(a)<f(b)
$$
for all $a\in A$ and $b\in B$.

= Finite-dimensional separation of open convex sets
{parent=Separation of a point and an open convex set}

If finitely many open convex subsets $K_1,\ldots,K_n$ of a locally convex space have empty intersection, some continuous linear map $T:X\to\mathbb R^{n-1}$ preserves that fact: $\bigcap_iT(K_i)=\varnothing$.

= Dual pair
{parent=Locally convex space}
{wiki}

= Separated dual pair
{synonym}

A dual pair $(E,F)$ consists of a real vector space $E$ and a vector space $F$ of linear functionals that separates the points of $E$. Its weak topology $\sigma(E,F)$ is the coarsest topology making every $f\in F$ continuous, and its continuous dual is exactly $F$.

= Continuous dual of a weak topology
{parent=Dual pair}
{title2=$(E,\sigma(E,F))'=F$}

For a <separated dual pair>, the continuous linear functionals for its <weak topology> are exactly evaluations by members of the paired space. Continuity bounds a functional on a neighbourhood defined by finitely many evaluations; scalar rescaling makes it vanish on their common kernel. It then factors through their finite-dimensional evaluation map and is a finite linear combination of them.

= Compact norming dual-pair criterion
{parent=Dual pair}

Let $Z\subseteq X^*$ separate points of a Banach space $X$. If the closed unit ball of $X$ is compact for $\sigma(X,Z)$ and $Z$ has the norm inherited from $X^*$, then the evaluation map $X\to Z^*$ is an isometric isomorphism. <Goldstine theorem> makes its unit-ball image weak-star dense, while the assumed compactness makes that image closed.

= Bipolar theorem for a dual pair
{c}
{parent=Dual pair}

For $A\subseteq E$ in a <dual pair> $(E,F)$, define $A^\circ=\{f\in F:f(a)\leq1\text{ for all }a\in A\}$. Then
$$
A^{\circ\circ}=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}^{\sigma(E,F)}(A\cup\{0\}).
$$

= Product of locally convex spaces
{parent=Locally convex space}

The product topology on $X\times Y$ is generated by the seminorms $(x,y)\mapsto p(x)$ and $(x,y)\mapsto q(y)$. Its continuous dual is canonically $X^*\oplus Y^*$.

= Continuous linear operator
{parent=Topological vector space}
{wiki}

= Continuous linear map
{synonym}

= Bounded linear operator
{synonym}

= Bounded operator
{synonym}

A continuous linear map is a <linear map> that is continuous for the topologies on its domain and codomain. Between <normed vector space>[normed vector spaces], continuity is equivalent to boundedness.

= Completely continuous operator
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$x_n\rightharpoonup0\Longrightarrow\|Tx_n\|\to0$}

A <bounded linear operator> between <Banach spaces> is completely continuous when it maps weakly convergent sequences to norm-convergent sequences. Equivalently, it maps weakly compact sets to norm-compact sets: the <Eberlein-Šmulian theorem> gives the forward direction, while a weakly convergent sequence together with its limit is weakly compact and gives the reverse direction. The inclusion $C(\mathbb T)\to L^2(\mathbb T)$ for finite <Lebesgue measure> is completely continuous by pointwise convergence, the <uniform boundedness principle> and the <dominated convergence theorem>, but is not a <compact operator> because of the orthogonal Fourier modes.

= Approximate surjectivity with geometric correction
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$Tx=y,\quad\|x\|\le R\|y\|/(1-k)$}

Let $T:V\to W$ be a <bounded linear operator> with $V$ a <Banach space>. If every $y\in W$ has an approximate preimage $x$ with $\|x\|\le R\|y\|$ and $\|Tx-y\|\le k\|y\|$, where $k<1$, then every $y$ has an exact preimage with the displayed norm bound. Repeatedly correct the residual: the corrections have norms at most $Rk^j\|y\|$ and form an absolutely convergent series; its image under $T$ equals $y$. This proves surjectivity but does not provide a bounded linear right inverse.

= Absolutely p-summing operator
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$\pi_p(T)$}

= p-summing operator
{synonym}

For $1\le p<\infty$, a <bounded linear operator> $T:E\to F$ between <Banach spaces> is absolutely p-summing if $(\sum_j\|Tx_j\|^p)^{1/p}\le C\sup_{\phi\in B_{E^*}}(\sum_j|\phi(x_j)|^p)^{1/p}$ for every finite family. The least $C$ is its p-summing norm $\pi_p(T)$. Direct substitution gives the ideal inequality $\pi_p(BTA)\le\|B\|\pi_p(T)\|A\|$ for compatible <bounded linear operators>.

= 2-summing operator
{parent=Absolutely p-summing operator}
{title2=$\pi_2(T)$}

A <bounded linear operator> $T:E\to F$ is 2-summing if $\sum_j\|Tx_j\|^2\le C^2\sup_{\phi\in B_{E^*}}\sum_j|\phi(x_j)|^2$ for every finite family; $\pi_2(T)$ is the least $C$. For operators between <Hilbert spaces>, this is equivalent to being a <Hilbert-Schmidt operator>, with $\pi_2(T)=\|T\|_{\mathrm{HS}}$. The upper bound follows by applying the <Hilbert-Schmidt norm> ideal inequality to the column operator of the family; testing finite orthonormal families gives the reverse bound.

= 2-summing norm of a finite-dimensional identity
{parent=2-summing operator}
{title2=$\pi_2(I_E)=\sqrt{\dim E}$}

For a finite-dimensional <normed vector space> $E$, represent a finite family by its column operator $A:\ell_2^m\to E$. Its operator norm is the family's weak square norm. Since $A=AQ$ for the <orthogonal projection> onto $(\ker A)^\perp$, summing $\|Ae_j\|^2\le\|A\|^2\|Qe_j\|_2^2$ proves the upper bound $\sqrt{\dim E}$. In coordinates adapted to a maximal inscribed Euclidean or Hermitian <ellipsoid>, the <John contact decomposition> gives $\sum_r c_ru_ru_r^*=I$ and $\sum_rc_r=\dim E$. The vectors $\sqrt{c_r}u_r$ have total squared norm $\dim E$ and weak square norm one, giving equality. This holds over both real and complex scalars.

= Pietsch factorization theorem
{c}
{parent=Absolutely p-summing operator}

For an <absolutely p-summing operator> $T:E\to F$, there is a <probability measure> $\mu$ on the dual unit ball with its <weak-star topology> such that $\|Tx\|\le\pi_p(T)(\int|\phi(x)|^p\,d\mu(\phi))^{1/p}$. Thus $T$ factors through the closed span of the evaluation functions in $L^p(\mu)$, with the final operator having norm at most $\pi_p(T)$. Conversely this factorization implies the summing inequality by summing under the integral. Existence of $\mu$ follows from finite-dimensional <Hahn-Banach separation theorem>: failure for finitely many vectors would contradict the summing inequality for a nonnegative weighted family. Compactness of probability measures then combines the finite constraints. The final operator need only be defined on the indicated closed subspace, not on all of $L^p(\mu)$.

= Absolutely summing operator
{parent=Absolutely p-summing operator}
{title2=$\pi_1(T)$}

= 1-summing operator
{synonym}

An absolutely summing operator is an <absolutely p-summing operator> with $p=1$. The identity on the infinite-dimensional <l2 sequence space> is not absolutely summing: the first $n$ coordinate vectors have sum of norms $n$, while their weak 1-norm is $\sqrt n$.

= Strictly singular operator
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

A bounded linear operator is strictly singular if it is bounded below on no infinite-dimensional subspace. Equivalently, every infinite-dimensional subspace contains unit vectors with arbitrarily small images. On a <hereditarily indecomposable Banach space>, an operator which is arbitrarily small on unit vectors in every finite-codimensional subspace is strictly singular: construct an infinite basic span on which its operator norm is small, then use the zero-angle property of hereditary indecomposability.

= Positivity-preserving linear operator on L1
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$u\ge0\Longrightarrow Tu\ge0$}

= Positivity-preserving operator
{synonym}

A bounded <linear operator> $T:L^1(\Omega)\to L^1(\Omega)$ preserves positivity when it takes nonnegative functions to nonnegative functions. By linearity it also preserves the pointwise order: $u\le v$ implies $Tu\le Tv$. For example, integration against a nonnegative kernel preserves positivity, as does the identity operator. This is order positivity, which differs from a positive quadratic form on a <Hilbert space>.

= Mean control for positive imaging operators
{parent=Positivity-preserving linear operator on L1}
{title2=$u_\Omega\|T1\|_1\le\|Tu\|_1+C_\Omega\|T\|\operatorname{TV}(u)$}

On a bounded connected <Lipschitz domain>, let $u\ge0$ belong to the <BV space>, and let $T$ be a <positivity-preserving operator> with $T1\ne0$. Writing $u=c1+(u-c1)$, $c=u_\Omega\ge0$, the <triangle inequality> and <Poincaré inequality for total variation> give $c\|T1\|_1\le\|Tu\|_1+\|T\|\|u-c\|_1\le\|Tu\|_1+C_\Omega\|T\|\operatorname{TV}(u)$. Thus forward-image and variation bounds control the missing constant mode, and hence the full $BV$ <norm>.

= Schur test
{c}
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$\|A\|_{\ell^2\to\ell^2}\le\sqrt{RC}$}
{wiki}

For a matrix with nonnegative entries, row sums at most $R$ and column sums at most $C$ imply an $\ell^2$ operator <norm> at most $\sqrt{RC}$. Indeed the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $|\sum_j A_{ij}x_j|^2\le(\sum_jA_{ij})\sum_jA_{ij}|x_j|^2$. Summing over $i$ bounds the result by $RC\sum_j|x_j|^2$. Integral kernels and positive weight functions give analogous forms. The symmetric case has equal row and column bounds.

= Bounded inverse
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$K^{-1}$}

An invertible <bounded linear operator> has a <bounded inverse> when $\|u\|\le C\|Ku\|$ for all $u$.

= Positive linear operator on continuous functions
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$f\geq0\Rightarrow Uf\geq0$}

= Positive linear operators on continuous functions
{synonym}

A linear map $U:C(K,\mathbb R)\to C(K,\mathbb R)$ is positive when $f\geq0$ implies $Uf\geq0$. This is positivity for the pointwise order, not positivity of a quadratic form. On a compact space, $|Uf|\leq U|f|\leq\|f\|_\infty U1$, so $\|U\|=\|U1\|_\infty$. This makes positive maps especially useful for <uniform approximation>.

= Zero-preserving linear operator on continuous functions
{parent=Positive linear operator on continuous functions}
{title2=$Z_f\subseteq Z_{Lf}\implies Lf=(L1)f$}

If a linear operator on real continuous functions preserves every zero, evaluate $f-f(x)1$ at $x$. The zero-preserving hypothesis forces $Lf(x)=f(x)L1(x)$, so the operator is multiplication by the continuous function $L1$. Positivity further makes that multiplier nonnegative; linearity and zero preservation alone already give the multiplication representation.

= Contractive linear operator
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$\|T\|\leq1$}

A bounded <linear operator> between normed spaces is contractive if its operator norm is at most one. This norm property is distinct from a strict <contraction mapping>, whose Lipschitz constant must be less than one.

= Range of a bounded linear operator
{parent=Continuous linear operator}
{title2=$\mathcal R(K)$}

= Operator range
{synonym}

The <operator range> is the <image of a linear map> $K$. It need not be closed in an infinite-dimensional <Hilbert space>; that failure makes the <Moore–Penrose inverse of an operator> unbounded on its domain.

= Extension of a bounded linear operator from a dense subspace
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

Let $D$ be a <dense subset>[dense linear subspace] of a normed space $X$, let $Y$ be a <Banach space>, and let $T:D\to Y$ be linear with $\lVert Tx\rVert\leq C\lVert x\rVert$. Then $T$ has a unique bounded extension $\widetilde T:X\to Y$, defined by $\widetilde Tx=\lim_nTx_n$ for any sequence $x_n\in D$ converging to $x$, and $\lVert\widetilde T\rVert\leq C$.

= Failure of dense-subspace extension into an incomplete codomain
{parent=Extension of a bounded linear operator from a dense subspace}

Let $c_{00}$ have the $\ell^2$ norm, take $D=c_{00}\subset X=\ell^2$, and let $T:D\to c_{00}$ be the identity. It is bounded but has no continuous extension $\ell^2\to c_{00}$, because continuity and density would force the extension to be the identity on every element of $\ell^2$.

= Banach space of bounded linear operators
{title2=$\mathcal B(X,Y)$}
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

If $X$ is a normed vector space and $Y$ is a <Banach space>, then the bounded linear maps $\mathcal B(X,Y)$ form a Banach space under the <operator norm>. Completeness of the domain is unnecessary.

= Continuous linear functional
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

= Bounded linear functional
{synonym}

A continuous linear functional is a continuous linear map from a <topological vector space> to its scalar field. The collection of all such functionals is the <continuous dual space>.

= Linear functional with closed kernel
{parent=Continuous linear functional}
{title2=$l\text{ continuous}\iff\ker l\text{ closed}$}

A <linear functional> on a <normed vector space> is continuous exactly when its kernel is closed. The forward direction follows because zero is closed in the scalar field. Conversely, if $l\ne0$, choose $v$ with $l(v)=1$ and write $M=\ker l$. Closedness gives $d=\operatorname{dist}(v,M)>0$. Since $x/l(x)-v\in M$ when $l(x)\ne0$, $|l(x)|\leq\|x\|/d$. The zero functional is continuous separately. Thus the criterion does not require completeness or the <closed graph theorem>.

= Point evaluation functional
{parent=Continuous linear functional}

= Point evaluation
{synonym}

For a function space on a set $X$ and a point $x\in X$, the point evaluation functional is $\delta_x(f)=f(x)$. It is bounded on $C(X)$ with the <supremum norm>.

= Sequential continuity criterion for a linear map on a metrizable topological vector space
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

A <linear map> from a <metrizable topological vector space> is continuous exactly when it maps every sequence converging to zero to a sequence converging to zero. If continuity fails, a decreasing countable neighborhood basis supplies a null sequence whose images stay outside one fixed neighborhood of zero.

= Isomorphism of Banach spaces
{parent=Continuous linear operator}

An isomorphism of Banach spaces is a bijective bounded <linear map> whose inverse is bounded. The <bounded inverse theorem> makes boundedness of the inverse automatic.
